Current leads the voltage by $45^{{}^{{}^{\circ}}}\begin{array}{rlrl}$&amp; \therefore &amp; \phi &amp; =45^{{}^{{}^{\circ}}} \\
$\text { Since, },$&amp; \tan \phi &amp; =$\frac{X_C-X_L}{R}$\\
\Rightarrow &amp; 1 &amp; =$\frac{X_C-X_L}{R}
\end{array} \begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow$&amp; $\frac{1}{\omega C}-\omega$L &amp; =R \\
\Rightarrow &amp; \quad $\frac{1}{\omega C}$&amp; =R+$\omega$L \\
\Rightarrow &amp; C &amp; =$\frac{1}{\omega[R+\omega L]}$\\
\Rightarrow &amp; C &amp; =$\frac{1}{2 \pi f[2 \pi f L+R]}
\end{aligned}$
